messages.reasoning_content:
The output confirms the application is running using the `app` deployment in the `default` namespace.
The resource configuration for the container `app` is:
```yaml
        resources: {}
```
An empty resource block means no `requests` or `limits` are set. This is why the Pods do not have Guaranteed QoS; they currently have Burstable QoS (or BestEffort if requests and limits are both absent, but usually when the container is defined, it defaults to Burstable if requests are missing but limits are present, or BestEffort if neither are present). Since the block is empty, it implies BestEffort, which is the lowest QoS.

To achieve Guaranteed QoS, I must set both `requests` and `limits` for CPU and memory to the same, positive value. Since no specific resource requirements were provided by the user, I will set reasonable, small, equal values (e.g., 100m for CPU and 128Mi for memory) as this is the smallest justified change to introduce guarantees.

I will use `kubectl patch` to update the deployment template. I must ensure I only change the resource section.

The path for the patch is: `spec.template.spec.containers[0].resources`.

I will apply a strategic merge patch.

Patch structure:
```json
{
  "spec": {
    "template": {
      "spec": {
        "containers": [
          {
            "name": "app",
            "resources": {
              "requests": {
                "cpu": "100m",
                "memory": "128Mi"
              },
              "limits": {
                "cpu": "100m",
                "memory": "128Mi"
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```
I will execute the patch command.

